  "insights": [
   "The US Treasury's surprise decision to increase long-end buybacks by $16bn per quarter ($64bn annually) marks a significant shift in debt management strategy.",
   "The announcement was timed outside the standard Quarterly Refunding process to maximize market impact ahead of the FOMC minutes release.",
   "The buyback increase will be funded via T-bills, which shortens the Treasury's Weighted Average Maturity (WAM) at the margin, though the impact is estimated to be minimal.",
   "Treasury is signaling a more activist, tactical approach to debt management, using communication as a tool to contain long-end yields.",
   "The intervention raises the bar for market participants to maintain short rates positions and may limit long-end yield increases in the near term.",
   "Treasury's activist approach risks eroding institutional credibility and the benefits of a predictable framework, potentially leading to higher term premia over time.",
   "The bank notes that Treasury's firepower is inherently more limited than the Fed's, and successive interventions may suffer from diminishing marginal effects.",
